- 001 «si en porta menys d’1 any» (×2, FAQ + body) — writing «menys d’un any» removes two `1` tokens and the checker reports `numbers FAIL missing from translation (2): 1×2`. Reverted to the digit form. (The adjacent «menys d’un any» the reviewer contrasted it with is unchanged, so the inconsistency the reviewer saw remains, forced by the checker.)
- 008 «es paga 1 mes de salari mitjà per cada 1 any complet» (×2, FAQ + body) — «un mes … per cada any complet» gives `numbers FAIL missing from translation (4): 1×4`. Reverted to the digit form.
- 004 link label (ca-a 18) — dropping «l’article 10» entirely gives `numbers FAIL missing from translation (1): 10`, because the Korean source label cites «영업세법 제10조». Kept the digit inside a clarification that now reads as information for the reader rather than an editor’s note to self.

## Where I think the reviewer is wrong (not applied)
- ca-a 7 also asks for the masculine pronoun in the disclaimer heading «La relació entre advocada o advocat i client no neix per si sola». There the subject is «la relació», a feminine noun, so «per si sola» is the correct agreement. Only the two sentences whose subject is an infinitive clause were changed.
- ca-b 11 asks to end the 015 sentence with a comma so that it “runs on into the next sentence”. The two sentences are separate Markdown blocks and the checker pins block counts, so they cannot be joined. The comma was applied (it matches the Korean source, which also ends that block with «…에 따르면,»), but the reader still sees two paragraphs; a real fix means merging the blocks in `src/content/columns/015-*.md` first.
- ca-a 44 asks to delete a sentence. Block counts are pinned, so the sentence was rewritten instead of deleted; the redundancy is gone but the block is still there.
- Not disagreements, but recorded: ca-a 16, ca-a 17 and ca-b 12 describe real defects that live in the Korean source (`src/content/columns/`), not in the Catalan. They cannot be fixed inside a translation lane.

